WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.310
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan here

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:05.190
and welcome to Middle School Biology.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:09.720
I have Iman Howard here who
manages our STEM content.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.120
Welcome Iman, and why should folks care

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.430
about middle school biology?

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.250
- Biology is the study of life,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.070
and so in this course it really answers

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.960
why are things the way they are?

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.580
Why do you look the way they do?

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.290
Why do I look the way I do?

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:29.370
And how every organism on earth
has a really special purpose

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.400
and a special trait, and
based on these traits,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.860
it helps them interact
with their environment.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.510
We even talk about energy,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.540
and energy can't be created
and it can't be destroyed.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.960
It actually cycles as
matter through an ecosystem.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.390
And it even has this really
cool chemical reactions

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.510
called photosynthesis where
plants use light energy

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:52.230
from the sun and they make
their own food, which is insane.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.510
I think about organisms like the gorilla

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.370
that are super big and strong,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.410
and they don't go and eat a cow.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.490
They go and eat plants

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:03.120
because that's like getting
energy right from the source.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.700
And then finally, we talk about evolution.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.090
Based on our traits, how
have we changed over time?

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.540
And how some of our natural
traits were selected

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.360
by the environment that
create those changes.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.470
Or even how humans,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.690
we have something called
artificial selection

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.670
which explains why your dog's so cute,

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.720
whether it's 100 pounds or
five pounds, we did that.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.120
What do you think is cool about it, Sal?

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.210
- Oh, well, you know,
biology is trying to answer,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.800
I mean, to your point,
the questions of life,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.630
which we still don't
understand a lot about,

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.520
but like what makes us us,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.890
and to realize that we don't exist

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.180
as human beings in isolation,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.260
we're part of an ecosystem.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.670
We're part of a story,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.140
a narrative that's been going on

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.840
for millions and billions of years.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.480
The fact that we're made
up of trillions of cells,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.660
each of them with DNA that
can describe who we are.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.910
How did all of this complexity come about?

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.890
This is maybe the most mind-blowing topic

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.870
that we're really at just the
beginning of this journey.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.990
So I think folks are gonna
be really, really excited,

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.370
or it's gonna be really interesting

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.980
to see all of the things
that they get to learn.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.030
- Well, let's dig in.

